          <p class="text-slate-light text-sm max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
            🔗 All infra control-plane comms (CrowdSec LAPI, Wazuh manager &amp; indexer, Grafana,
            Prometheus, and SSH) ride the Tailscale mesh with strict ACLs and per-session SSH auth.
            Hosts firewall with <span class="text-accent font-semibold">nftables</span>, SSH is key-only, and the
            only deliberate public surfaces are the threat-feed endpoint, the isolated Kasm VM, and the
            ScreenConnect session relay. Every admin console stays on the tailnet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-slate leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto mt-10">
          <p>
            Security runs as a <span class="text-accent font-semibold">distributed CrowdSec</span> deployment:
            log processors across the hosts and edge parse logs and ship alerts to a central
            <span class="text-accent font-semibold">LAPI</span>, a Postgres-backed, LAPI-only Security Engine
            that is the single source of truth, while bouncers query it for decisions. Because the
            processors don't talk to each other, the central API is what makes a detection on one box
            actionable everywhere, and all of that traffic stays on Tailscale.
          </p>
          <p>
            The engine's full decision union (local bans plus the CAPI community feed and console lists,
            around 75k entries) is mirrored on the public <span class="text-accent font-semibold">thallium</span>
            box and served as a plaintext list at
            <span class="text-accent font-semibold">threat.cktechnology.io/crowdsec.txt</span>, which the
            FortiGate pulls in as an external threat feed and enforces with a DENY policy. An attack on
            the public Azure website is reported by its bouncer, lands in the feed, and is blocked at the
            perimeter for every other service, closing the detection-to-enforcement loop. DNS is just as
            deliberate: Technitium and Pi-hole both front a validating Unbound resolver, with FortiGate
            SD-WAN failing over between them so name resolution survives a resolver going down.
          </p>
